   "why_feature_variants_are_pinned_here": "These are NOT dark and are NOT part of the set above — they execute today. They are pinned into the DEFINED population because both of the other sources for a binary's feature dimension are artifacts that a deletion PR deletes. `rust_test features` was the only source, so a commit removing //tools/buck:auth-rest-dev-auth-inline-postgres from ci.yml together with its sh_test and its rust_test — the ordinary shape of a wrapper-removal PR — dropped this binary from `defined` and from `executed` in the same instant. The dark set did not move, the gate exited 0, and an entire dev-auth PostgreSQL suite stopped running with no diagnostic. Pinned, the binary survives its own deletion, goes dark, and names itself. The current entries include ordinary database, Auth development, and dedicated app recovery variants; a pinned entry that stops being `defined` is reported UNRESOLVED, so this list cannot rot into decoration.",
   "test_attribute_baseline_note": "This map is a cheap lexical deletion ratchet over reachable source files, not a runtime case count. It counts declared #[test], #[tokio::test], and #[sqlx::test] attributes without evaluating cfg expressions, feature selection, macro expansion, or #[ignore]. A source reached by multiple feature-bearing binaries appears once; exact (source, feature set) binary reachability is enforced separately by the named dark-set ratchet. For example, console-app's bare and test-postgres binaries list 153 and 18 tests respectively in the compiler harness, while this static source metric records 171 attributes once.",
